#!/bin/sh
#
# @name VTOL Tricopter
#
# @type VTOL Tiltrotor
#
# Tricopter VTOL in the style of the E-flite Convergence (PX4's real-hardware
# airframe 13012, which has never shipped a simulation model): three tilting
# rotors -- two front, one rear. Derived from 4020_gz_tiltrotor -- see
# Tools/simulation/gz/models/tricopter, which is the stock quad tiltrotor
# model with one rear rotor removed and the other moved to the centreline.
#
# The rear rotor tilts too (CA_ROTOR2_TILT, tilt servo 3 below): vertical for
# takeoff/landing/hover like the front pair, tilted aft as an active pusher
# in cruise. All three rotors share equal thrust (CA_ROTOR2_CT, see the
# comment there) -- the rear was originally sized to half the vehicle's
# weight, a "cruise pusher only needs to beat drag" assumption that caused a
# real yaw-right-at-takeoff bug (see that comment). Unlike the front two the
# rear is NOT wired into any differential tilt-control axis (CA_SV_TL2_CT =
# None) -- see that param below for why.
#
# Yaw in hover comes from DIFFERENTIAL TILT of the two front rotors
# (CA_SV_TL0/1_CT = Yaw), not from rotor drag-torque differential: with a
# single rear rotor its reaction torque is inherently unbalanced and has to
# be trimmed continuously by the tilt servos. That is also how the real
# Convergence flies. Two firmware constraints, verified against
# src/modules/control_allocator/VehicleActuatorEffectiveness/
# ActuatorEffectivenessTiltrotorVTOL.cpp (and ActuatorEffectivenessTilts.cpp)
# at the PX4 commit this workspace is pinned to, shape the rear tilt's
# config below and are worth knowing before changing it:
#
#   1. A rotor only auto-stops in forward flight if it has NO tilt servo at
#      all (CA_ROTORi_TILT=None puts it in the firmware's untiltable-motor
#      mask, which is what zeroes it in FlightPhase::FORWARD_FLIGHT). A
#      rotor that IS tiltable -- front pair included -- keeps spinning in
#      cruise; there is no PX4 param to have a rotor be both tiltable (for
#      hover yaw, here) and auto-stopped in FW. So the front pair keep
#      contributing thrust in cruise alongside the new rear pusher; making
#      them untiltable to force a literal stop would cost them their only
#      hover yaw mechanism.
#   2. Pitch-tilt-control is hardcoded OFF for this vehicle type:
#      getEffectivenessMatrix() always calls
#      _tilts.updateTorqueSign(geometry, /*disable_pitch=*/true), and that
#      function only applies a tilt's pitch torque when disable_pitch is
#      false. CA_SV_TLi_CT = Pitch (or Yaw+Pitch) is therefore a silent
#      no-op on this firmware -- CT = Yaw is the only differential-control
#      mode that actually does anything for a tilt here. The rear tilt uses
#      CT = None rather than claim a pitch-control effect that won't exist;
#      its thrust still gets pitch authority for free from plain geometry
#      (CA_ROTOR2_PX puts it off the CG on the X axis), no CT setting needed
#      for that part.
#
# SCALE IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE REAL AIRCRAFT'S. The real E-flite Convergence
# is 0.962 kg / 0.762 m span / ~0.14 m^2 wing (specs recovered from the
# ArduPilot SITL_Models RealFlight model, which is the only Convergence asset
# that exists anywhere -- no Gazebo/gz model has ever shipped, in PX4's full
# git history or elsewhere). This model keeps the 5.115 kg template-derived
# scale instead, and only adopts the Convergence LAYOUT. Two reasons: the
# rotor positions here (+-0.35 m track) already sit close to the real 0.762 m
# span, and this project's entire calibration -- wind-field magnitudes, the
# 12 m/s cruise, canyon dimensions, CBF margins, the trained wind estimator --
# assumes a ~5 kg vehicle. A 0.96 kg foam airframe is thrown around far more
# by the same wind field, which would change the experiment itself. Rescale
# only alongside re-deriving all of that (and re-tuning the yaw loop below).
#

. ${R}etc/init.d/rc.vtol_defaults

PX4_SIMULATOR=${PX4_SIMULATOR:=gz}
PX4_GZ_WORLD=${PX4_GZ_WORLD:=default}
PX4_SIM_MODEL=${PX4_SIM_MODEL:=tricopter}

param set-default SIM_GZ_EN 1 # Gazebo bridge

param set-default MAV_TYPE 21

param set-default CA_AIRFRAME 3

# Geometry matches the model.sdf link poses, converted from Gazebo FLU
# (x fwd, y left) to PX4 FRD (x fwd, y right): front pair at x=+0.35,
# y=+-0.35; rear rotor on the centreline at x=-0.35, y=0.
param set-default CA_ROTOR_COUNT 3
# All three CT below are PX4's own thrust coefficient (module.yaml:
# "Thrust = CT * u^2"). That description is about the rotor's REAL curve,
# used e.g. by the hover-thrust estimator's physical intuition -- it is
# NOT what the control allocator itself uses. The allocator's effectiveness
# matrix (ActuatorEffectivenessRotors::addActuatorsMultirotor) is linear in
# u (thrust = ct*u, not ct*u^2), and critically, unlike the roll/pitch/yaw
# rows, the Z-thrust row is NEVER column-normalized
# (ControlAllocationPseudoInverse.cpp hardcodes
# `_control_allocation_scale(THRUST_Z) = 1.f`) -- so CT's absolute value is
# a direct, unnormalized gain between mc_pos_control's hover-relative
# thrust setpoint (~[0,1], 1.0 meaning "MPC_THR_HOVER's worth of collective
# thrust") and the actual per-rotor actuator command u. A REAL bug was
# found live going the OTHER direction from what you'd guess: setting CT to
# the rotor's actual real-Newtons max thrust (matching model.sdf's
# MulticopterMotorModel: motorConstant * maxRotVelocity^2 = 1.072e-05 *
# 1500^2 = 24.12) made the allocator solve for near-zero u for any normal
# thrust setpoint (verified live: actuator_motors control ~0.0004 at a
# commanded 0.8 climb, vehicle stuck at idle never really leaving the
# ground) -- the opposite failure mode from the ORIGINAL bug below (that
# one undersized CT and saturated every motor at max, still short of
# weight; this one oversized it and commands ~0 thrust everywhere). CT
# has to be picked empirically for the vehicle to actually fly, not derived
# from either "ratio-preserve the stock default" or "match real Newtons" --
# both are plausible-sounding and both are wrong for this allocator. 4.0
# was found live to give a good margin (climbs cleanly, no rotor pinned at
# a limit under a moderate climb command); retune here first if hover
# feels sluggish (raise CT) or twitchy/saturating (lower it), together with
# MPC_THR_HOVER below.
#
# Earlier (ORIGINAL) bug, kept for history: an even-earlier version of
# this file set CT by scaling PX4's stock default (6.5) by the ratio of
# model.sdf's motorConstant to a stock value (6.5 * 7.504e-06/2e-05 =
# 2.44) -- undersized enough that the vehicle armed and the land-detector
# flickered takeoff/landing, but it never actually left the ground.
#
# Also real, separately: the rear motor used to be undersized to half the
# front motors' thrust (a "cruise pusher only needs to beat drag, not full
# weight" assumption that doesn't apply here -- this project never uses
# the fixed-wing cruise phase, ENABLE_VTOL_TRANSITION=False in
# controller_node) -- it saturated during the climb-out throttle transient
# before the front tilt servos' differential-yaw correction caught up,
# producing a visible yaw-right at every takeoff. All three rotors now
# share one 1/3-of-weight thrust target.
# Rotor 0: front right, CCW, tilted by servo 1
param set-default CA_ROTOR0_PX 0.35
param set-default CA_ROTOR0_PY 0.35
param set-default CA_ROTOR0_KM 0.05
param set-default CA_ROTOR0_TILT 1
param set-default CA_ROTOR0_CT 4
# Rotor 1: front left, CW, tilted by servo 2
param set-default CA_ROTOR1_PX 0.35
param set-default CA_ROTOR1_PY -0.35
param set-default CA_ROTOR1_KM -0.05
param set-default CA_ROTOR1_TILT 2
param set-default CA_ROTOR1_CT 4
# Rotor 2: rear centre, CCW, now tiltable (servo 3) -- vertical for
# hover/takeoff/landing, tilted aft as a pusher in cruise.
param set-default CA_ROTOR2_PX -0.35
param set-default CA_ROTOR2_PY 0.0
param set-default CA_ROTOR2_KM 0.05
param set-default CA_ROTOR2_TILT 3
param set-default CA_ROTOR2_CT 4

# Real bug found live: this was never set, so it defaulted to PX4's stock
# MPC_THR_HOVER=0.5. The hover thrust estimator (mc_hover_thrust_estimator)
# is meant to correct this from real flight data, but it needs an actual
# liftoff to get a usable accel/thrust signal -- if the initial value is
# far enough off that the vehicle never leaves the ground, it's a stuck
# initial condition and the estimator never gets a chance to run. There
# isn't a clean closed-form value here the way there is for a normal
# multirotor: unlike CA_ROTOR_CT (which sets the allocator's actual
# thrust gain, see that comment), MPC_THR_HOVER only sets mc_pos_control's
# own idea of "1.0 collective = how much thrust", used to bias the initial
# guess before the estimator takes over -- it does not have to satisfy any
# exact weight/CT relationship, just be close enough to get the vehicle
# off the ground. 0.7 was confirmed live to do that with CA_ROTOR_CT=4
# above; if you retune CT, sanity-check this still gets a liftoff before
# trusting the estimator's corrections.
param set-default MPC_THR_HOVER 0.7

# Set explicitly (matches PX4's stock default here, but pinned so it can't
# silently drift on a PX4 upgrade) -- web_viewer/control_server.py's
# MAX_VELOCITY_MPS=3.0 assumes this ceiling; the model.sdf motorConstant
# was raised alongside it (~2.14x thrust/weight) so there's real thrust
# authority to actually reach and sustain 3 m/s, not just permission to
# ask for it.
param set-default MPC_Z_VEL_MAX_UP 3.0

# Tilt servos travel -15..90 deg rather than the stock 0..90. The negative
# end is what gives hover yaw authority in BOTH directions: at a vertical
# hover tilt the allocator has to be able to tilt one nacelle back as well as
# the other forward to trim the rear rotor's torque. The gz joint limits in
# model.sdf were widened to match. Normalised tilt therefore maps as
# 0 -> -15 deg, 1 -> +90 deg, which is what the VT_TILT_* values below encode.
param set-default CA_SV_TL_COUNT 3
param set-default CA_SV_TL0_MINA -15
param set-default CA_SV_TL0_MAXA 90
param set-default CA_SV_TL0_TD 0
param set-default CA_SV_TL0_CT 1  # Yaw -- re-enabled now that CA_ROTORi_CT is correctly tuned (was disabled while CT was still wrongly oversized at 24.12; see that comment)
param set-default CA_SV_TL1_MINA -15
param set-default CA_SV_TL1_MAXA 90
param set-default CA_SV_TL1_TD 0
param set-default CA_SV_TL1_CT 1  # Yaw -- re-enabled, see CA_SV_TL0_CT above
# Tilt 3 (rear/tail): same range and fore-aft plane as the front two, but
# CT = None (not Yaw or Pitch) -- see the header comment above for why
# (pitch-tilt-control is hardcoded off for this vehicle type in PX4, and
# this tilt isn't wired into yaw either, to leave the tuned front-pair yaw
# loop below untouched).
param set-default CA_SV_TL2_MINA -15
param set-default CA_SV_TL2_MAXA 90
param set-default CA_SV_TL2_TD 0
param set-default CA_SV_TL2_CT 0

param set-default CA_SV_CS0_TRQ_R -0.5
param set-default CA_SV_CS0_TYPE 1
param set-default CA_SV_CS1_TRQ_R 0.5
param set-default CA_SV_CS1_TYPE 2
param set-default CA_SV_CS2_TRQ_P 1
param set-default CA_SV_CS2_TYPE 3
param set-default CA_SV_CS_COUNT 3

param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_FUNC1 101
param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_FUNC2 102
param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_FUNC3 103

param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_MIN1 10
param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_MIN2 10
param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_MIN3 10

param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_MAX1 1500
param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_MAX2 1500
param set-default SIM_GZ_EC_MAX3 1500

param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_FUNC1 201
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_FUNC2 202
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_FUNC3 203
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_FUNC4 204
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_FUNC5 205
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_FUNC6 206

# Must match CA_SV_TL0/TL1/TL2 above -- these are the same three tilt servos
# seen from the simulator side (SIM_GZ_SV_* is 1-based, model.sdf's servo_N
# topics are 0-based, so channel 4 is servo_3, channel 5 is servo_4, and
# channel 6 is servo_5 -- previously an unused/commented-out rudder hookup
# in model.sdf, now the rear tilt servo).
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_MAXA4 90
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_MINA4 -15
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_MAXA5 90
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_MINA5 -15
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_MAXA6 90
param set-default SIM_GZ_SV_MINA6 -15

param set-default NPFG_PERIOD 12
param set-default FW_PR_FF 0.2
param set-default FW_PR_P 0.9
param set-default FW_PSP_OFF 2
param set-default FW_P_LIM_MIN -15
param set-default FW_RR_FF 0.1
param set-default FW_RR_P 0.3
param set-default FW_THR_TRIM 0.38
param set-default FW_THR_MAX 0.6
param set-default FW_THR_MIN 0.05
param set-default FW_T_CLMB_MAX 8
param set-default FW_T_SINK_MAX 2.7
param set-default FW_T_SINK_MIN 2.2

param set-default MC_AIRMODE 1
# Yaw gains history -- TWO different yaw actuation mechanisms have been
# tuned here at different times, and these gains only make sense for
# whichever one is CURRENTLY active (CA_SV_TL0/1_CT, above):
#   1. Tilt-servo yaw (CA_SV_TL0/1_CT=Yaw, current). Stock tiltrotor
#      default (P 0.4 / I 0.1) was tried first and spun the vehicle at
#      +-8..15 rad/s, sign flipping every cycle, 1050 m off course --
#      servos are a much lower-bandwidth actuator than the stock tiltrotor
#      assumes, and 0.4/0.1 way overshoots that bandwidth.
#   2. Differential-thrust yaw (CA_SV_TL0/1_CT=0/None, used temporarily
#      while tilt-yaw was disabled for an unrelated diagnostic). 0.075/
#      0.0125/0.0025 (rescaled /1.6 from a 0.12/0.02/0.004 baseline
#      alongside a 1.6x model.sdf motorConstant increase) was tuned for
#      THIS mechanism -- a much faster rotor-speed-based path than tilt
#      servos. Re-enabling tilt-yaw and testing live with these gains
#      still in place gave a small steady-state yaw drift: too weak to
#      hold heading tightly against the tilt servos' slower response, not
#      an oscillation. Retuned live for tilt-yaw, landing well short of
#      the stock 0.4/0.1 that caused the sign-flipping spin in (1): P 0.075
#      -> 0.16, I scaled proportionally 0.0125 -> 0.027, D left closer to
#      unchanged (0.0025 -> 0.004, D mostly damps rate noise, less tied to
#      which actuator produces the torque). If this drifts again, raise P
#      first in small steps; if it starts oscillating/hunting, you've
#      crossed back toward the servo-bandwidth wall from (1) and should
#      back off P before I.
param set-default MC_YAWRATE_P 0.16
param set-default MC_YAWRATE_I 0.027
param set-default MC_YAWRATE_D 0.004
param set-default MPC_MAN_Y_MAX 60
param set-default MC_YAWRATE_MAX 60

# Roll stability, raised above PX4's stock default (MC_ROLL_P 4.0,
# MC_ROLLRATE_P/I/D 0.15/0.2/0.003) -- unlike the yaw gains above, this
# wasn't chasing an instability, just tightening a request for less roll
# wander during manual flight. Roll (front-left/front-right rotor pair) is
# geometrically symmetric on this airframe, unlike pitch (front pair vs.
# the single rear rotor), so the stock quad-tuned defaults are a
# reasonable starting point here in a way they weren't for yaw's very
# different actuator (tilt servos, not rotor speed). Re-tune down if this
# starts hunting/oscillating rather than just holding tighter.
param set-default MC_ROLL_P 5.0
param set-default MC_ROLLRATE_P 0.2
param set-default MC_ROLLRATE_I 0.2
param set-default MC_ROLLRATE_D 0.004

# Pitch/roll limit. PX4's default 45 deg is what let the observed transits
# reach +45..47 deg of pitch. This is the ONLY enforced tilt bound in
# multicopter mode: controller_node publishes POSITION setpoints, and PX4's
# mc_pos_control derives the attitude from its own feedback, so neither that
# node nor the CBF (which filters only the added feedforward term, and only in
# treatment mode) can bound attitude -- the limit has to live here.
#
# NOTE it is symmetric TOTAL tilt (roll and pitch combined), so an asymmetric
# bound like -20/+30 is not expressible: that would need either a patch to
# mc_pos_control or this project taking over attitude control itself. In
# fixed-wing mode PX4 does have asymmetric FW_P_LIM_MIN/MAX, which is where
# such a constraint naturally belongs.
#
# Trade-off: tilt caps horizontal acceleration at g*tan(tilt) = 5.66 m/s^2 at
# 30 deg, against MPC_ACC_HOR's 3.0 budget -- so this bounds attitude without
# cutting into the acceleration the controller actually asks for.
param set-default MPC_TILTMAX_AIR 30

param set-default MPC_XY_VEL_P_ACC 3
param set-default MPC_XY_VEL_I_ACC 4
# Tried doubling to 8 as a control: does integrator retuning alone recover
# the FO-PINN's benefit, without any wind estimator? Measured, n=7 matched
# seeds: I=8 baseline 4.48 m vs I=4 baseline 4.33 m vs PINN(gain 0.2) 4.28 m --
# I=8 was the WORST of the three (not significant at this n, p=0.67-0.71, so
# take this as directional, not proof). Reverted to 4. The tracking error is
# genuinely slow (see controller_node's feedforward_gain comment -- 90% of
# its spectral power sits below 0.11 Hz), so integral action was the obvious
# thing to try, but simply pushing it harder does not reproduce what the PINN
# does. A full n=20 run would settle this properly if it matters later.
param set-default MPC_XY_VEL_D_ACC 0.1

param set-default MIS_TAKEOFF_ALT 10

param set-default VT_FWD_THRUST_EN 4
param set-default VT_FWD_THRUST_SC 0.6
param set-default VT_TYPE 1
# Normalised against the -15..90 deg servo range above:
#   MC    0.143 -> 0 deg   (all three rotors vertical in hover, tilt room both ways)
#   TRANS 0.571 -> 45 deg  (the partial-tilt accelerate phase)
#   FW    1.0   -> 90 deg  (fully forward/aft; front pair keep pulling, rear
#                           pushes -- see the header comment on why the rear
#                           doesn't (and the front pair can't cleanly) stop)
param set-default VT_TILT_MC 0.143
param set-default VT_TILT_TRANS 0.571
param set-default VT_TILT_FW 1.0

# SITL: disable preflight checks that don't apply to simulation.
# NOTE: do NOT disable SYS_HAS_MAG / SYS_HAS_BARO -- Gazebo simulates both
# sensors via the Magnetometer/AirPressure world plugins, and the EKF needs
# the simulated mag for yaw alignment ("no heading reference" otherwise).
# SYS_HAS_NUM_ASPD is 1: the world file loads gz-sim-air-speed-system, so the
# model's airspeed sensor actually publishes (see the aerocanyon repo's
# worlds/_template.sdf).
param set-default COM_ARM_WO_GPS 1
param set-default SYS_HAS_NUM_ASPD 1
param set-default CBRK_SUPPLY_CHK 894281
param set-default COM_DISARM_LAND 0
param set-default NAV_DLL_ACT 0
